{"acceleration_structures":[],"air_sha256":"7909d8bf653d00443177078a7127da4ab4fbdd19e0a1f267154d887eca22ec50","argument_buffer_buffers":[],"argument_buffer_intersection_function_tables":[],"argument_buffer_textures":[],"authored_by":"claude:opus-5","buffers":[{"binding":0,"initial_bytes_b64":"","role":"output"}],"case_id":"5816d98539355cee9dce9a9e8a0d8df8af2598789f530fb797bc9f390d9c47f8","compare":{"kind":"exact"},"device_buffer_arrays":[],"dispatch":{"grid":[8,1,1],"threads_per_threadgroup":[8,1,1]},"entry":"kernel_integer_width_conversions","execution_safety":"loop_free","function_constants":[],"intersection_function_tables":[],"kernel_stage_inputs":[],"name":"convert-integers-across-every-width","output":{"binding":0,"kind":"buffer","length":2880,"offset":0},"rationale":"Twenty-three uncovered air.convert.* families in one kernel, plus five that come free with them: air.convert.u.v4i16.u.v4i1 (2 case-less corpus sources), air.convert.u.v4i16.f.v4f16 (2), air.convert.u.v3i16.u.v3i8 (2), air.convert.s.v2i32.s.v2i8 (2), air.convert.s.v2i16.s.v2i32 (2), air.convert.f.v4f16.u.v4i16 (2) and seventeen more at one source each -- 29 sources. A width conversion has exactly two ways to be wrong and both are silent: it can sign-extend where it should zero-extend, and it can keep bits it should have dropped. Every input is chosen so both show. The narrowing sources overflow their destination on purpose -- t * 300 - 500 and -t * 70000 + 5 do not fit in 8 or 16 bits, so char4(i4) and short4(i4) must drop the high bits rather than clamp, and the surviving low bits differ per thread. Half the lanes are negative: t - 3 is negative for the first three threads and -t * 70000 for all but the first, so a widening that zero-extended a signed source, or a narrowing read back as signed where the symbol says unsigned, lands four orders of magnitude away rather than at a plausible neighbour. u4.w is 4294967040 + t, which is negative as an int32 and enormous as a uint32 -- the same disagreement at the other end, and its truncation to 65280 + t is the evidence the high bits went. Both signednesses of the same byte pattern are asked side by side: uchar4(i4) and char4(i4) must produce identical bytes under different symbols, and int4(uchar4(u4)) against int4(c4) separates zero- from sign-extension on the same width pair. The halves are all non-negative with a fraction of 3/4 or 1/2, so the float-to-integer direction truncates toward zero rather than rounding and the answer moves by one if it rounds. The integer-to-half direction is asked where it is exact: u4 masked to 16 bits gives 0, 300..2100 in steps of 300, 4464..31248 in steps of 4464 and 65280, and every one of those is a whole multiple of its binade's half spacing, so the whole vector is representable and a mismatch is a lowering difference rather than a rounding one. The 64-bit results are written as two 32-bit words each, low first, so a conversion that produced the right low word and the wrong sign extension is still visible; ulong4(u4) and ulong4(i4) are asked on the same lanes for exactly that. Every store is straight-line, so the module is loop-free.","render_targets":[],"samplers":[],"stage":"kernel","texture_arrays":[],"textures":[],"threadgroup_memory":[],"vertex_inputs":[],"visible_function_references":[],"visible_function_tables":[]}
